\tag{fdip}
\title{Grassmann variables and finite fermion integrals}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Integrating commuting Gaussian fields produces inverse powers
of a determinant. Anticommuting variables give a second finite
algebraic construction: their top coefficient produces a determinant
or a Pfaffian. This supplies the integration rule for quadratic
fermion expressions before choosing a lattice Dirac operator.}

\p{The [exterior algebra](ca-000E) fixes the signs, while an ordered
top word fixes the coefficient functional. Two generators make the
convention visible before the general formulas. The resulting Wick
rule uses inverse alternating matrices; its relationship with the
earlier [Clifford algebra](fdip-001B) is a vector-space comparison.}

